example.paragraph:
If $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      4 & 11 & 14 \\
      8 & 7 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, then the linear transformation $\textbf{x}\mapsto A\textbf{x}$ maps the unit sphere $\left\{ \textbf{x}:\text{∥}\textbf{x}\text{∥} = 1 \right\}$ in $\text{ℝ}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$, shown in Figure 1. Find a unit vector $\textbf{x}$ at which the length $\parallel A{\textbf{x} \parallel}$ is maximized, and compute this maximum length.

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{cl}
      \textbf{y} & {= A\textbf{x} = c_{1}A\textbf{v}_{1} + \cdots + c_{r}A\textbf{v}_{r} + c_{r + 1}A\textbf{v}_{r + 1} + \cdots + c_{n}A\textbf{v}_{n}} \\
       & {= c_{1}A\textbf{v}_{1} + \cdots + c_{r}A\textbf{v}_{r} + 0 + \cdots + 0}
      \end{array}$$

example.paragraph:
Use the results of Examples 1 and 2 to construct a singular value decomposition of $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      4 & 11 & 14 \\
      8 & 7 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.

Find a singular value decomposition of $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & {- 1} \\
      {- 2} & 2 \\
      2 & {- 2}
      \end{bmatrix}\,.$

Finally, set $U = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      \textbf{u}_{1} & \textbf{u}_{2} & \textbf{u}_{3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, take $\text{Σ}$ and $V^{T}$ from above, and write

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{ll}
      {U = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      U_{r} & U_{m - r}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,} & {\text{where~}U_{r} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \textbf{u}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{u}_{r}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
      {V = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      V_{r} & V_{n - r}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,} & {\text{where~}V_{r} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \textbf{v}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{v}_{r}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}
      \end{array}$$

$$A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      U_{r} & U_{m - r}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\begin{bmatrix}
      D & 0 \\
      0 & 0
      \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
      V_{r}^{T} \\
      V_{n - r}^{T}
      \end{bmatrix} = U_{r}DV_{r}^{T}$$

exercise_group.instructions:
Find an SVD of each matrix in Exercises 5–12. [Hint:In Exercise 11, one choice for $U$ is] $\begin{bmatrix}
      {- 1\text{/}3} & {2\text{/}3} & {2\text{/}3} \\
      {2\text{/}3} & {- 1\text{/}3} & {2\text{/}3} \\
      {2\text{/}3} & {2\text{/}3} & {- 1\text{/}3}
      \end{bmatrix}\,.$ In Exercise 12, one column of $U$ can be $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{6}} \\
      {- 2\text{/}\sqrt{6}} \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{6}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$.﻿

statement.paragraph:
Suppose the factorization below is an SVD of a matrix $A$, with the entries in $U$ and $V$ rounded to two decimal places.$$\begin{array}{l}
      {A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      .40 & {- .78} & .47 \\
      .37 & {- .33} & {- .87} \\
      {- .84} & {- .52} & {- .16}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      7.10 & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & 3.10 & 0 \\
      0 & 0 & 0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
      {\mspace{27mu} \times \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      .30 & {- .51} & {- .81} \\
      .76 & .64 & {- .12} \\
      .58 & {- .58} & .58
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}
      \end{array}$$

Repeat Exercise 15 for the following SVD of a $3 \times 4$ matrix $A$:$$\begin{array}{l}
      {A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      {- .86} & {- .11} & {- 50} \\
      .31 & .68 & {- .67} \\
      .41 & {- .73} & {- .55}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{llll}
      12.48 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & 6.34 & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & 0 & 0 & 0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
      {\mspace{27mu} \times \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrrr}
      .66 & {- .03} & {- .35} & .66 \\
      {- .13} & {- .90} & {- .39} & {- .13} \\
      .65 & .08 & {- .16} & {- .73} \\
      {- .34} & .42 & {- .84} & {- .08}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}
      \end{array}$$

Let $U = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \textbf{u}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{u}_{m}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $V = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \textbf{v}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{v}_{n}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, where the $\textbf{u}_{i}$ and $\textbf{v}_{i}$ are as in Theorem 10. Show that$$A = \sigma_{1}\textbf{u}_{1}\textbf{v}_{1}^{T} + \sigma_{2}\textbf{u}_{2}\textbf{v}_{2}^{T} + \cdots + \sigma_{r}\textbf{u}_{r}\textbf{v}_{r}^{T}$$
